 as sp\nfrom itertools import product\n\n# Define the expression components\nterms = [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]\noperators = ['*', '*', '*', '+']\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with given parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(terms, operators, parentheses):\n    expression = str(terms[0])\n    for i in range(len(operators)):\n        if parentheses[i] == 1:\n            expression += '(' + str(operators[i]) + ' ' + str(terms[i+1]) + ')'\n        else:\n            expression += str(operators[i]) + ' ' + str(terms[i+1])\n    expression += str(operators[-1]) + ' ' + str(terms[-1])\n    return sp.sympify(expression)\n\n# Generate all possible placements of parentheses\ndef generate_parentheses(terms, operators):\n    n = len(terms) - 1\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        yield [0] * (n - 1)\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            yield [0] * (i - 1) + [1] + [0] * (n - i - 1)\n\n# Get all distinct values\ndistinct_values = set()\nfor parentheses in generate_parentheses(terms, operators):\n    value = evaluate_expression(terms, operators, parentheses)\n    distinct_values.add(value)\n\n# Count the number of distinct values\nnum_distinct_values = len(distinct_values)\nprint(num_distinct_values)\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nIt seems that the code is not correctly generating all possible placements of parentheses and evaluating them. Let's correct the approach and ensure that we consider all possible ways to insert parentheses into the expression.\n\nWe need to consider all possible ways to split the expression into sub-expressions and evaluate each combination. We can use a recursive approach to handle this.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression components\nterms = [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]\noperators = ['*', '*', '*', '+']\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with given parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(terms, operators, start, end, parentheses):\n    if start == end:\n        return terms[start]\n    if parentheses[start] == 1:\n        return evaluate_expression(terms, operators, start + 1, end, parentheses) * terms[start]\n    if start == 0:\n        return terms[start] * evaluate_expression(terms, operators, start + 1, end, parentheses)\n    return terms[start] * evaluate_expression(terms, operators, start + 1, end, parentheses)\n\n# Generate all possible placements of parentheses\ndef generate_parentheses(terms, operators, start, end):\n    if start == end:\n        return [0]\n    placements = []\n    for i in range(start, end):\n        placements.append([0] * (end - start))\n        placements[-1][i - start] = 1\n    return placements\n\n# Get all distinct values\ndistinct_values = set()\nfor parentheses in generate_parentheses(terms, operators, 0, len(terms) - 1):\n    value = evaluate_expression(terms, operators, 0, len(terms) - 1, parentheses)\n    distinct_values.add(value)\n\n# Count the number of distinct values\nnum_distinct_values = len(distinct_values)\nprint(num_distinct_values)\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nIt seems that the approach still isn't correctly generating all possible placements of parentheses and evaluating them. Let's take a different approach and manually consider all possible ways to insert parentheses into the expression $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1$.\n\nWe can manually list out all possible ways to insert parentheses and then evaluate each expression. Here are the possible placements of parentheses:\n\n1. $((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1$\n2. $(2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4)) \\cdot 5 + 1$\n3. $(2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1$\n4. $2 \\cdot ((3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5) + 1$\n5. $2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5)) + 1$\n6. $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1$\n7. $(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1$\n8. $2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5) + 1$\n9. $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1$\n\nNow let's evaluate each of these expressions:\n\n1. $((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1 = (6 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1 = 24 \\cdot 5 + 1 = 120 + 1 = 121$\n2. $