e the length of \\(DF\\) as \\(h\\). Then:\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{7}{h} = 0.7\n\\]\nFrom this, we can solve for \\(h\\):\n\\[\nh = \\frac{7}{0.7} = 10\n\\]\nNow we have the hypotenuse \\(DF = 10\\). To find \\(DE\\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nDF^2 = DE^2 + EF^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[\n10^2 = DE^2 + 7^2\n\\]\n\\[\n100 = DE^2 + 49\n\\]\n\\[\nDE^2 = 100 - 49 = 51\n\\]\n\\[\nDE = \\sqrt{51}\n\\]\nSo the length of \\(DE\\) is \\(\\sqrt{51}\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given values\nEF = 7\nsin_D = 0.7\n\n# Calculate the hypotenuse DF\nDF = EF / sin_D\n\n# Use the Pythagorean theorem to find DE\nDE = math.sqrt(DF**2 - EF**2)\nprint(DE)\n```\n```output\n7.14142842854285\n```\nThe length of \\(DE\\) is \\(\\sqrt{51}\\), which is approximately 7.14142842854285. Therefore, the exact length of \\(DE\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{51}}\n\\]", "To find the length of \\(DE\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\), we can use the definition of the sine function in a right triangle. The sine of an angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. In this case, \\(\\sin D = \\frac{EF}{DF} = 0.7\\). We know that \\(EF = 7\\), so we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{7}{DF} = 0.7\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can solve for \\(DF\\):\n\n\\[\nDF = \\frac{7}{0.7} = 10\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the hypotenuse \\(DF\\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of \\(DE\\):\n\n\\[\nDF^2 = DE^2 + EF^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[\n10^2 = DE^2 + 7^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n100 = DE^2 + 49\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(DE\\):\n\n\\[\nDE^2 = 100 - 49 = 51\n\\]\n\n\\[\nDE = \\sqrt{51}\n\\]\n\nSo the length of \\(DE\\) is \\(\\sqrt{51}\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given values\nEF = 7\nsin_D = 0.7\n\n# Calculate the hypotenuse DF\nDF = EF / sin_D\n\n# Calculate DE using the Pythagorean theorem\nDE = math.sqrt(DF**2