 in the given right triangle \\( \\triangle DEF \\), we can use the definition of sine in a right triangle. The sine of an angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( \\sin D = 0.7 \\)\n- \\( EF = 7 \\) (which is the side opposite to angle \\( D \\))\n- \\( DF \\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle\n\nFrom the definition of sine:\n\\[ \\sin D = \\frac{EF}{DF} = \\frac{7}{DF} = 0.7 \\]\n\nWe can solve for \\( DF \\):\n\\[ DF = \\frac{7}{0.7} = 10 \\]\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\( DE \\):\n\\[ DE^2 + EF^2 = DF^2 \\]\n\\[ DE^2 + 7^2 = 10^2 \\]\n\\[ DE^2 + 49 = 100 \\]\n\\[ DE^2 = 100 - 49 \\]\n\\[ DE^2 = 51 \\]\n\\[ DE = \\sqrt{51} \\]\n\nSo, the length of \\( DE \\) is \\( \\sqrt{51} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given values\nsin_D = 0.7\nEF = 7\n\n# Calculate the hypotenuse DF\nDF = EF / sin_D\n\n# Calculate DE using the Pythagorean theorem\nDE = math.sqrt(DF**2 - EF**2)\nprint(DE)\n```\n```output\n7.14142842854285\n```\nThe length of \\( DE \\) is approximately \\( 7.14142842854285 \\), which is \\(\\sqrt{51}\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{51}}\n\\]", "To find the length of \\(DE\\) given that \\(\\sin D = 0.7\\) and \\(EF = 7\\), we can use the definition of sine in a right triangle. In the right triangle \\(DEF\\), \\(\\sin D\\) is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the hypotenuse. Here, the opposite side to angle \\(D\\) is \\(EF\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(DF\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{EF}{DF} = 0.7\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(EF = 7\\), we can substitute this value into the equation:\n\n\\[\n0.7 = \\frac{7}{DF}\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can solve for \\(DF\\):\n\n\\[\nDF = \\frac{7}{0.7} = 10\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the length of \\(DE\\). Since \\(DF\\) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle \\(DEF\\) and \\(EF\\) i