} = 72^\\circ \\).\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. A regular pentagon has 5 sides.\n2. When a regular pentagon is rotated around its center, it will coincide with its original position after every \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\) rotation.\n3. Therefore, the minimum number of degrees it must be rotated until it coincides with its original position is \\(72^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code using sympy, although this problem doesn't necessarily require symbolic computation.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the total degrees in a circle\ntotal_degrees = 360\n\n# Define the number of sides of the pentagon\nsides = 5\n\n# Calculate the minimum angle of rotation\nmin_rotation = total_degrees / sides\n\n# Print the result\nprint(min_rotation)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\) degrees.", "To determine the minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position, we need to understand the symmetry of the pentagon. A regular pentagon has rotational symmetry of order 5, meaning it can be rotated by \\( \\frac{360^\\circ}{5} = 72^\\circ \\) and still look the same.\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of degrees the pentagon must be rotated until it coincides with its original position is \\( 72^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code snippet to ensure accuracy. We'll calculate the angle of rotation for a regular pentagon.\n```python\n# Calculate the minimum angle of rotation for a regular pentagon\nnum_sides = 5\nangle_of_rotation = 360 / num_sides\nprint(angle_of_rotation)\n```\n```output\n72.0\n```\nThe minimum number of degrees a regular pentagon must be rotated counterclockwise about its center until it coincides with its original position is \\(\\boxed{72}\\)