f unity).\n\nThe 6th roots of unity are the roots of the equation \\( z^6 - 1 = 0 \\), which are \\( 1, e^{2\\pi i / 6}, e^{4\\pi i / 6}, e^{6\\pi i / 6}, e^{8\\pi i / 6}, e^{10\\pi i / 6} \\). Simplifying the exponents, we get \\( 1, e^{\\pi i / 3}, e^{2\\pi i / 3}, -1, e^{4\\pi i / 3}, e^{5\\pi i / 3} \\). The roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( e^{\\pi i / 3}, e^{2\\pi i / 3}, e^{4\\pi i / 3}, e^{5\\pi i / 3} \\), which are indeed the 6th roots of unity (excluding 1 and -1).\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( 12 \\), because the 6th roots of unity are also 12th roots of unity. To confirm, we can check that the 12th roots of unity include all the 6th roots of unity.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\n\n# Define the polynomial z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\ndef polynomial(z):\n    return z**4 + z**2 + 1\n\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\nroots = [cmath.rect(1, (2 * cmath.pi * k) / 6) for k in [1, 2, 4, 5]]\n\n# Check if these roots are 12th roots of unity\nis_12th_root_of_unity = all(abs(z**12 - 1) < 1e-10 for z in roots)\n\nprint(is_12th_root_of_unity)\n```\n```output\nTrue\n```\nThe Python code confirms that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are indeed 12th roots of unity. Therefore, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. An \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) root of unity is a complex number \\( z \\) such that \\( z^n = 1 \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can make a substitution \\( w = z^2 \\), which transforms the equation into \\( w^2 + w + 1 = 