6\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute \\(\\alpha^9\\), \\(\\alpha^6\\), and \\(\\alpha^3\\) into the equation \\(\\alpha^9 + p\\alpha^6 + q\\alpha^3 + r = 0\\):\n\\[\n83\\alpha^2 + 76\\alpha - 16 + p(-9\\alpha^2 - 11\\alpha + 4) + q(3\\alpha^2 - 4\\alpha + 1) + r = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n83\\alpha^2 + 76\\alpha - 16 - 9p\\alpha^2 - 11p\\alpha + 4p + 3q\\alpha^2 - 4q\\alpha + q + r = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(83 - 9p + 3q)\\alpha^2 + (76 - 11p - 4q)\\alpha + (-16 + 4p + q + r) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince this must hold for all roots \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\), the coefficients must be zero:\n\\[\n83 - 9p + 3q = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n76 - 11p - 4q = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n-16 + 4p + q + r = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations step by step. First, solve the first two equations for \\(p\\) and \\(q\\):\n\\[\n83 - 9p + 3q = 0 \\implies 9p - 3q = 83\n\\]\n\\[\n76 - 11p - 4q = 0 \\implies 11p + 4q = 76\n\\]\n\nMultiply the first equation by 4 and the second equation by 3 to eliminate \\(q\\):\n\\[\n36p - 12q = 332\n\\]\n\\[\n33p + 12q = 228\n\\]\n\nAdd these two equations:\n\\[\n69p = 560 \\implies p = \\frac{560}{69}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(p = \\frac{560}{69}\\) into \\(9p - 3q = 83\\):\n\\[\n9\\left(\\frac{560}{69}\\right) - 3q = 83 \\implies \\frac{5040}{69} - 3q = 83 \\implies 3q = \\frac{5040}{69} - 83 \\implies 3q = \\frac{5040 - 5727}{69} \\implies 3q = \\frac{-687}{69} \\implies q = \\frac{-229}{69}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(p = \\frac{560}{69}\\) and \\(q = \\frac{-229}{69}\\) into \\(-16 + 4p + q + r = 0\\):\n\\[\n-16 + 4\\left(\\frac{560}{69}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{-229}{69}\\right) + r = 0 \\implies -16 + \\frac{2240}{69} - \\frac{229}{69} + r = 0 \\implies -16 + \\frac{2011}{69} + r = 0 \\implies r = 16 - \\frac{2011}{69} \\implies r = \\frac{1104 - 2011}{69} \\implies r = \\frac{-907}{69}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ordered triple \\((p, q, r)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{560}{69}, \\frac{-229}{69}, \\frac{-907}{69}\\right)\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom 