onometric identities to express (sin(x))^7 in terms of sin(7x), sin(5x), sin(3x), and sin(x)\n# We need to find the coefficients a, b, c, and d such that (sin(x))^7 = a*sin(7x) + b*sin(5x) + c*sin(3x) + d*sin(x)\n# We can use the known identities and compare the coefficients\n\n# Expand (sin(x))^7 using trigonometric identities\nsin_x_7_expanded = sp.expand_trig(sin_x_7)\n\n# Compare the coefficients of sin(7x), sin(5x), sin(3x), and sin(x)\ncoeffs = sp.collect(sin_x_7_expanded, [sin_7x, sin_5x, sin_3x, sin_x], evaluate=False)\n\n# Extract the coefficient of sin(x)\nd = coeffs[sin_x]\n\nprint(d)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nThe output indicates that the coefficient \\(d\\) is \\(0\\). This means that the term \\(\\sin x\\) does not appear in the expansion of \\((\\sin x)^7\\) when expressed in terms of \\(\\sin 7x\\), \\(\\sin 5x\\), \\(\\sin 3x\\), and \\(\\sin x\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(d\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "To find the value of \\(d\\) in the given equation \\((\\sin x)^7 = a \\sin 7x + b \\sin 5x + c \\sin 3x + d \\sin x\\), we can use the method of comparing the coefficients of the Fourier series expansion of \\((\\sin x)^7\\).\n\nWe start by expressing \\((\\sin x)^7\\) in terms of complex exponentials using Euler's formula:\n\\[\n\\sin x = \\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\left(\\frac{e^{ix} - e^{-ix}}{2i}\\right)^7 = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} (e^{ix} - e^{-ix})^7.\n\\]\nExpanding the binomial \\((e^{ix} - e^{-ix})^7\\) gives:\n\\[\n(e^{ix} - e^{-ix})^7 = \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (e^{ix})^{7-k} (-e^{-ix})^k = \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x}.\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x} = \\frac{1}{(2i)^7} \\left( \\sum_{k=0}^7 \\binom{7}{k} (-1)^k e^{i(7-2k)x} \\right).\n\\]\nSimplifying the factor \\(\\frac{1}{(2i)^7}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{(2i)^7} = \\frac{1}{2^7 i^7} = \\frac{1}{128 (-i)} = \\frac{i}{128}.\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\n(\\sin x)^7 = \\frac{i}{128}