iginal square is \\( s^2 \\).\n\nWhen two opposite sides of the square are decreased by 40%, their new length becomes \\( s \\times (1 - 0.40) = 0.60s \\). When the other two sides are increased by 50%, their new length becomes \\( s \\times (1 + 0.50) = 1.50s \\).\n\nThe area of the resulting rectangle is the product of these new side lengths: \\( 0.60s \\times 1.50s = 0.90s^2 \\).\n\nThe area of the rectangle is \\( 0.90s^2 \\), which is 90% of the original square's area \\( s^2 \\). This means the area has decreased by \\( 100\\% - 90\\% = 10\\% \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the original side length of the square\r\ns = 1  # The actual value of s doesn't matter because we're dealing with percentages\r\n\r\n# Calculate the new side lengths\r\nnew_side1 = s * (1 - 0.40)  # 60% of the original side length\r\nnew_side2 = s * (1 + 0.50)  # 150% of the original side length\r\n\r\n# Calculate the original and new areas\r\noriginal_area = s ** 2\r\nnew_area = new_side1 * new_side2\r\n\r\n# Calculate the percentage decrease in area\r\npercentage_decrease = ((original_area - new_area) / original_area) * 100\r\nprint(percentage_decrease)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe area of the square decreases by \\(\\boxed{10}\\) percent when the lengths of two opposite sides are decreased by 40% and the lengths of the other two sides are increased by 50%.", "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the area of the original square and the area of the resulting rectangle after modifying the side lengths. Then, we can determine the percentage decrease in the area.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Let the side length of the original square be \\( s \\). The area of the original square is \\( s^2 \\).\n2. After modifying the side lengths, the new rectangle will have side lengths \\( 0.6s \\) and \\( 1.5s \\). The area of the resulting rectangle is \\( 0.6s \\times 1.5s = 0.9s^2 \\).\n3. The decrease in area is \\( s^2 - 0.9s^2 = 0.1s^2 \\).\