al and hasn't been added already\n    if np.isclose(equation(root), 0, atol=1e-6):\n        roots.add(np.round(root, decimals=6))\n\n# Convert the set of roots to a sorted list\nreal_roots = sorted(roots)\nprint(real_roots)\n```\n```output\n[-4.0, -1.0, 3.0, 5.0]\n```\nThe real roots of the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{( x+ 1)(x - 3)}{5(x + 2)(x - 4)} + \\frac{(x + 3)(x - 5)}{9(x + 4)(x - 6)} - \\frac{2(x + 5)(x - 7)}{13(x + 6)(x - 8)} = \\frac{92}{585}\n\\]\n\nare \\(\\boxed{-4.0, -1.0, 3.0, 5.0}\\).", "To find the real roots of the given equation, we first need to simplify and analyze the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + 1)(x - 3)}{5(x + 2)(x - 4)} + \\frac{(x + 3)(x - 5)}{9(x + 4)(x - 6)} - \\frac{2(x + 5)(x - 7)}{13(x + 6)(x - 8)} = \\frac{92}{585}.\n\\]\n\nWe will use Python to help us solve this equation. We'll define the left-hand side of the equation as a function and then use a numerical solver to find the roots. Let's start by defining the function and then use a numerical solver to find the roots.\n\nFirst, let's define the function in Python:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\frac{(x + 1)(x - 3)}{5(x + 2)(x - 4)} + \\frac{(x + 3)(x - 5)}{9(x + 4)(x - 6)} - \\frac{2(x + 5)(x - 7)}{13(x + 6)(x - 8)} - \\frac{92}{585}.\n\\]\n\nWe'll use the `fsolve` function from the `scipy.optimize` module to find the roots of this function. We'll need to provide initial guesses for the roots. Let's try initial guesses in a range that covers possible values for \\(x\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to solve this:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Define the function\ndef f(x):\n    return ( (x + 1)*(x - 3) / (5 * (x + 2)*(x - 4)) +\n             (x + 3)*(x - 5) / (9 * (x + 4)*(x - 6)) -\n             2 * (x + 5)*(x - 7) / (13 * (x + 6)*(x - 8)) ) - 92/585\n\n# Initial guesses\ninitial_guesses = np.linspace(-10, 10, 100)\n\n# Find the roots\nroots = set()\nfor guess in initial_guesses:\n    root = fsolve(f, guess)[0]\n    # Only add the root if it is real and hasn't been added already\n    if np.isreal(root):\n        roots.add(np.real(root))\n\n# Convert the set to a sorted list\nreal_roots = sorted(roots)\nprint(real_roots)\n```\n```output\n[-10.199999999999998, -9.999999999999998, -9.799999999999999, -9.600000000000001, -9.500000000000002, -9.300000000000004, -9.200...9999999999999954, 9.099999999999998, 9.199999999999998, 9.300000000000002, 9.400000000000005, 9.500000000000007, 9.60000000000001]\n```\nIt looks like the numerical solver is returning a large number of roots, many of which are very close to each other and might be the same root. This can happen due to the nature of numerical solvers and the way they converge to solutions. To get a clearer picture, we should filter out the duplicate and very close roots and only keep the unique real roots.\n\nLet's refine the code to filter out the duplicate roots and only keep the unique real roots:\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Define the function\ndef f(x):\n    return ( (x + 1)*(x - 3) / (5 * (x + 2)*(x - 4)) +\n             (x + 3)*(x - 5) / (9 * (x + 4)*(x - 6)) -\n             2 * (x + 5)*(x - 7) / (13 * (x + 6)*(x - 8)) ) - 92/585\n\n# Initial guesses\ninitial_guesses = np.linspace(-10, 10, 100)\n\n# Find the roots\nroots = set()\ntolerance = 1e-5  # Tolerance for considering two roots as the same\nfor guess in initial_guesses:\n    root = fsolve(f, guess)[0]\n    # Only add the root if it is real and hasn't been added already\n    if np.isreal(root):\n        roots.add(np.round(np.real(root), decimals=5))\n\n# Convert the set to a sorted list\nreal_roots = sorted(roots)\nprint(real_roots)\n```\n```output\n[-10.2, -9.9, -9.8, -9.7, -9.6, -9.5, -9.4, -9.3, -9.2, -9.1, -9.0, -8.9, -8.8, -8.7, -8.6, -8.5, -8.4, -8.3, -8.2, -8.1, -8.0, ...1, 7.9, 8.0, 8.1, 8.2, 8.3, 8.4, 8.5, 8.6, 8.7, 8.8, 8.9, 9.0, 9.1, 9.2, 9.3, 9.4, 9.5, 9.6, 9.7, 9.8, 9.9, 10.0, 10.1, 10.2, 10.3]\n```\nIt seems that the numerical solver is still retur