:\n\n1. The known angles are \\(105^\\circ\\), \\(110^\\circ\\), and the opposite angle to \\(\\alpha\\) (which we will call \\(\\beta\\)). The opposite angle to \\(105^\\circ\\) is \\(\\beta\\), and the opposite angle to \\(110^\\circ\\) is the angle opposite to \\(\\alpha\\), which we will call \\(\\gamma\\).\n\n2. The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\). So, the sum of the known angles plus the unknown angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\) is \\(720^\\circ\\).\n\n3. Since the hexagon is cyclic, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, \\(\\alpha + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\\) and \\(105^\\circ + \\beta = 180^\\circ\\). From this, we can find \\(\\beta = 180^\\circ - 105^\\circ = 75^\\circ\\).\n\n4. Now we can find \\(\\gamma\\) using the fact that the sum of the interior angles of the hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + \\alpha + \\gamma + \\beta = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\\) and \\(\\beta = 75^\\circ\\), we can substitute \\(\\gamma = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + \\alpha + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) + 75^\\circ = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + 180^\\circ + 75^\\circ = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n545^\\circ = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThis equation is true, so we can solve for \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha + \\gamma = 180^\\circ \\implies \\alpha + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gamma = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\\), we can find \\(\\alpha\\) by subtracting the known angles from \\(360^\\circ\\) (since the sum of the angles around a point is \\(360^\\circ\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = 360^\\circ - (105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ) = 360^\\circ - 290^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(70^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Know