bda = 4$ satisfies the condition.\n\nTherefore, the only value of $\\lambda$ that satisfies the condition is $\\lambda = 4$.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{4}\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection of the two given curves and then compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\).\n\nThe equations of the curves are:\n1. \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\)\n2. \\(x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46 = 0\\)\n\nFirst, we will solve these equations simultaneously to find the points of intersection. We can subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate the \\(x^2\\) and \\(6x\\) terms:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72) - (x^2 - y^2 + 6x + 16y - 46) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2y^2 - 40y + 118 = 0\n\\]\n\nDividing through by 2, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^2 - 20y + 59 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -20\\), and \\(c = 59\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{400 - 236}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{164}}{2} = \\frac{20 \\pm 2\\sqrt{41}}{2} = 10 \\pm \\sqrt{41}\n\\]\n\nSo the solutions for \\(y\\) are \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\) and \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\).\n\nNext, we substitute these \\(y\\) values back into one of the original equations to find the corresponding \\(x\\) values. We'll use the first equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6x - 24y + 72 = 0\\):\n\nFor \\(y = 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + (10 + \\sqrt{41})^2 + 6x - 24(10 + \\sqrt{41}) + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 100 + 20\\sqrt{41} + 41 + 6x - 240 - 24\\sqrt{41} + 72 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 + (-4\\sqrt{41}) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x - 27 - 4\\sqrt{41} = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 6\\), and \\(c = -27 - 4\\sqrt{41}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 4(27 + 4\\sqrt{41})}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 + 108 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2} = \\frac{-6 \\pm \\sqrt{144 + 16\\sqrt{41}}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo the solutions for \\(x\\) are \\(x = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\) and \\(x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}\\).\n\nSimilarly, for \\(y = 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\), we get the same \\(x\\) values but with the opposite sign for the square root term:\n\n\\[\nx = -3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = -3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}\n\\]\n\nSo the points of intersection are:\n\n\\[\n\\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 + \\sqrt{41}}, 10 + \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 + 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right), \\left(-3 - 2\\sqrt{9 - \\sqrt{41}}, 10 - \\sqrt{41}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to compute the sum of the distances from these points to the point \\((-3, 2)\\). The distance formula is:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nFor each point \\((x, y)\\), the distance to \\((-3, 2)\\) is:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's compute these distances and sum them up. We'll use Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the points of intersection\r\nx1, y1 = -3 + 2*sp.sqrt(9 + sp.sqrt(41)), 10 + sp.sqrt(41)\r\nx2, y2 = -3 - 2*sp.sqrt(9 + sp.sqrt(41)), 10 + sp.sqrt(41)\r\nx3, y3 = -3 + 2*sp.sqrt(9 - sp.sqrt(41)), 10 - sp.sqrt(41)\r\nx4, y4 = -3 - 2*sp.sqrt(9 - sp.sqrt(41)), 10 - sp.sqrt(41)\r\n\r\n# Define the point (-3, 2)\r\nx0, y0 = -3, 2\r\n\r\n# Calculate the distances\r\nd1 = sp.sqrt((x1 - x0)**2 + (y1 - y0)**2)\r\nd2 = sp.sqrt((x2 - x0)**2 + (y2 - y0)**2)\r\nd3 = sp.sqrt((x3 - x0)**2 + (y3 - y0)**2)\r\nd4 = sp.sqrt((x4 - x0)**2 + (y4 - y0)**2)\r\n\r\n# Sum the distances\r\ntotal_distance = d1 + d2 + d3 + d4\r\nprint(total_distance.evalf())\n```\n```out