unt = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\ntrailing_zeroes_42_factorial = count_trailing_zeroes(42)\nprint(trailing_zeroes_42_factorial)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) (42 factorial) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", 'Given the equations:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i = 96, \\quad \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 = 144, \\quad \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 = 216,\n\\]\nwe can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to find the possible values of \\(n\\).\n\nThe Cauchy-Schwarz inequality states that:\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\\right)^2 \\leq n \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2.\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n96^2 \\leq n \\cdot 144.\n\\]\nSimplifying this, we have:\n\\[\n9216 \\leq 144n \\implies n \\geq \\frac{9216}{144} = 64.\n\\]\nNext, we use the relationship between the power sums of the sequence. We can use the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\\right)^3 - 3 \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\\right) \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2\\right) + 3 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k.\n\\]\nGiven that \\(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = 96\\), \\(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 = 144\\), and \\(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = 216\\), we can substitute these values into the identity:\n\\[\n216 = 96^3 - 3 \\cdot 96 \\cdot 144 + 3 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k.\n\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n216 = 884736 - 41472 + 3 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n216 = 843264 + 3 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k.\n\\]\nSo:\n\\[\n3 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k = 216 - 843264 = -843048.\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\n\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} a_i a_j a_k = -281016.\n\\]\nSince the sum of the products of the terms taken three at a time is negative, it suggests that the terms \\(a_i\\) must be negative, which contradicts the fact that they are positive real numbers. Therefore, we need to re-evaluate the constraints and consider the possibility of all terms being equal.\n\nIf all \\(a_i\\) are equal, let \\(a_i = a\\). Then:\n\\[\nna = 96 \\implies a = \\frac{96}{n}.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = \\frac{96}{n}\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\nn \\left(\\frac{96}{n}\\right)^2 = 144 \\implies \\frac{9216}{n} = 144 \\implies n = 64.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = \\frac{96}{64} = \\frac{3}{2}\\) into the third equation:\n\\[\n64 \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^3 = 64 \\cdot \\frac{27}{8} = 216.\n\\]\nThis confirms that \\(n = 64\\) is a valid solution. Since we have shown that \\(n \\geq 64\\) and that \\(n = 64\\) satisfies all the given conditions, the only possible value for \\(n\\) is 64. Therefore, the sum of all possible values of \\(n\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}.\n\\]', "To solve the problem, we will use the given conditions and the properties of the sequences. Let's denote the sequence by $(a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n)$. The given conditions are:\n1. $\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = 96$\n2. $\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 = 144$\n3. $\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = 216$\n\nWe will use the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\\[ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\right)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 + 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\]\n\\[ 96^2 = 144 + 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\]\n\\[ 9216 = 144 + 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\]\n\\[ 9072 = 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\]\n\\[ \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j = 4536 \\]\n\nNext, we will use the identity for the sum of cubes again:\n\\[ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 + \\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\\[ 96 \\cdot 144 = 216 + \\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\\[ 13824 = 216 + \\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\\[ 13608 = \\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\nWe can express $\\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j)$ as:\n\\[ \\sum_{1 \\leq i \\neq j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) = \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) + \\sum_{1 \\leq j < i \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\cdot a_i + \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j \\cdot a_j \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\sum_{1 \\leq j \\leq n, j \\neq i} a_j a_i \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\left( \\sum_{1 \\leq j \\leq n} a_j a_i - a_i^2 \\right) \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\left( \\sum_{1 \\leq j \\leq n} a_j a_i - a_i^2 \\right) \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\left( 96 a_i - a_i^2 \\right) \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n (96 a_i^2 - a_i^3) \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( 96 \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 - \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( 96 \\cdot 144 - 216 \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\left( 13824 - 216 \\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2 \\cdot 13608 \\]\n\\[ = 27216 \\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[ 13608 = 27216 \\]\nThis is a contradiction, so we need to check if there are any other possible values for $n$. We will use the fact that the sum of the squares of the sequence is fixed and the sum of the cubes is fixed. We will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to find the possible values of $n$.\n\nThe Cauchy-Schwarz inequality states that:\n\\[ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\right)^2 \\leq n \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 \\]\n\\[ 96^2 \\leq n \\cdot 144 \\]\n\\[ 9216 \\leq 144n \\]\n\\[ n \\geq 64 \\]\n\nWe also know that:\n\\[ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n 1^2 \\right) \\geq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\right)^2 \\]\n\\[ 144n \\geq 96^2 \\]\n\\[ 144n \\geq 9216 \\]\n\\[ n \\geq 64 \\]\n\nWe also know that:\n\\[ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 \\right) \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\right) \\l