4} = a\\). Then:\n\\[\n64a = 96 \\implies a = \\frac{96}{64} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nChecking the quadratic mean:\n\\[\n64a^2 = 144 \\implies a^2 = \\frac{144}{64} = \\frac{9}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nChecking the cubic mean:\n\\[\n64a^3 = 216 \\implies a^3 = \\frac{216}{64} = \\frac{27}{8} \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSince all conditions are satisfied, \\(n = 64\\) is a valid solution. We need to check if there are any other possible values of \\(n\\).\n\nLet's consider if \\(n > 64\\). If \\(n > 64\\), then the arithmetic mean would be:\n\\[\n\\frac{96}{n} < \\frac{96}{64} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nBut this would contradict the fact that the quadratic mean is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\) (since the quadratic mean is always greater than or equal to the arithmetic mean). Therefore, \\(n > 64\\) is not possible.\n\nThus, the only possible value of \\(n\\) is 64. The sum of all possible values of \\(n\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given conditions\nsum_a = 96\nsum_a_squared = 144\nsum_a_cubed = 216\n\n# Calculate the possible value of n\nn = sum_a ** 2 / sum_a_squared\n\n# Check if n is an integer and satisfies all conditions\nif n.is_integer():\n    n = int(n)\n    # Check if the conditions are satisfied for n\n    a = sum_a / n\n    if sum_a_squared == n * a ** 2 and sum_a_cubed == n * a ** 3:\n        possible_n = n\n    else:\n        possible_n = None\nelse:\n    possible_n = None\n\nprint(possible_n)\n```", "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\(n\\) given the following conditions:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = 96, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 = 144, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = 216.\n\\]\nLet's denote the sum of the \\(a_i\\) by \\(S_1\\), the sum of the squares of the \\(a_i\\) by \\(S_2\\), and the sum of the cubes of the \\(a_i\\) by \\(S_3\\). So we have:\n\\[\nS_1 = 96, \\quad S_2 = 144, \\quad S_3 = 216.\n\\]\nWe can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and the power mean inequality to help us fi