 = -29\n\\]\n\nSo the point on the curve at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\((15, -29)\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the parameter t\nt = 2\n\n# Calculate x and y using the given parameterized equations\nx = t**3 + 7\ny = -3*t**2 - 6*t - 5\n\nprint(((x, y)))\n```\n```output\n(15, -29)\n```\nThe point on the curve at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(15, -29)}\\).", "To find the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nThe parameterized equations are:\n\\[ x = t^3 + 7 \\]\n\\[ y = -3t^2 - 6t - 5 \\]\n\nLet's substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into these equations.\n\n1. Calculate \\( x \\) when \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[ x = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15 \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( y \\) when \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[ y = -3(2^2) - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29 \\]\n\nSo the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( (15, -29) \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the parameterized equations\ndef x(t):\n    return t**3 + 7\n\ndef y(t):\n    return -3*t**2 - 6*t - 5\n\n# Substitute t = 2 into the equations\nt = 2\nx_value = x(t)\ny_value = y(t)\n\nprint(((x_value, y_value)))\n```\n```output\n(15, -29)\n```\nThe point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(15, -29)}\\).", "To find the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations \\( x = t^3 + 7 \\) and \\( y = -3t^2 - 6t - 5 \\).\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15. \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = -3(2^2) - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29. \\]\n\nSo, the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( (15, -29) \\).\n\nLet's confirm this by running the Python code.\n```python\n# Define the parameter t\nt = 2\n\n# Calculate x a