 start by finding the GCD of 100 and 150. We can use the Euclidean algorithm for this purpose.\n\nThe prime factorization of 100 is \\(2^2 \\cdot 5^2\\), and the prime factorization of 150 is \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5^2\\). The GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both numbers, which is \\(2 \\cdot 5^2 = 50\\).\n\nNext, we list all the factors of 50: 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50. Among these, the two-digit factors are 10, 25, and 50.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to find all factors of a number\ndef find_factors(n):\n    factors = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            factors.append(i)\n    return factors\n\n# Find the GCD of 100 and 150\ngcd = math.gcd(100, 150)\n\n# Find all factors of the GCD\nfactors_of_gcd = find_factors(gcd)\n\n# Filter out the two-digit factors\ntwo_digit_factors = [factor for factor in factors_of_gcd if 10 <= factor < 100]\n\n# The number of two-digit factors\nnum_two_digit_factors = len(two_digit_factors)\nprint(num_two_digit_factors)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n2. List all the factors of the GCD.\n3. Count how many of these factors are two-digit numbers.\n\nFirst, we find the GCD of 100 and 150. The prime factorizations of 100 and 150 are:\n\\[ 100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2 \\]\n\\[ 150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2 \\]\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor appearing in the factorizations:\n\\[ \\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2 \\times 5^2 = 50 \\]\n\nNext, we list all the factors of 50:\n\\[ 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50 \\]\n\nAmong these factors, the two-digit numbers are 1