t is \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\). To rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), giving us \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nSo, the simplified expression is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this step-by-step using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression\nexpr = sp.sqrt(sp.root(sp.sqrt(1/729), 3))\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expr = sp.simplify(expr)\n\n# Rationalize the denominator\nrationalized_expr = sp.together(simplified_expr)\n\nprint(rationalized_expr)\n```\n```output\n0.577350269189626\n```\nThe numerical value of the expression \\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}}}\\) is approximately \\(0.577350269189626\\). This value is equivalent to \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\), as we derived earlier.\n\nSo, the simplified expression with a rational denominator is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\\).", "To simplify the expression \\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}}}\\), we can break it down step by step.\n\n1. Start with the innermost expression \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}\\).\n2. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}\\).\n3. Take the cube root of the result from step 2.\n4. Finally, take the square root of the result from step 3.\n\nLet's do this step by step.\n\n1. Simplify \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{729}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{729}} = \\frac{1}{27}\n   \\]\n2. Take the cube root of \\(\\frac{1}{27}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n3. Take the square root of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n   \\]\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\nSo the final simplified expression is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\