 third side c\npossible_c = []\nfor c in range(1, 10):  # We can safely assume c is within this range for this problem\n    if a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a:\n        possible_c.append(c)\n\nprint(possible_c)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe only possible integer value for the third side \\(c\\) that satisfies the triangle inequality theorem is 3. Therefore, the length of the third side is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the length of the third side of a triangle with sides measuring 1 unit and 3 units, we need to use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nIn this case, let the sides of the triangle be \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c\\) be the unknown third side. The inequalities become:\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > c\\) ⟹ \\(4 > c\\) ⟹ \\(c < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + c > 3\\) ⟹ \\(c > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + c > 1\\) (This is always true for positive \\(c\\), so it doesn't provide new information)\n\nSo, combining the inequalities, we get \\(2 < c < 4\\). Since \\(c\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(c\\) is 3. Let's verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the sides of the triangle\na = 1\nb = 3\n\n# Find the possible integer values for the third side c\npossible_values = []\nfor c in range(1, 5):  # We only need to check from 1 to 4\n    if a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a:\n        possible_values.append(c)\n\nprint(possible_values)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe only possible integer value for the third side of the triangle is \\(3\\). Therefore, the length of the third side is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the length of the third side of the triangle, we can use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a