le inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nLet's denote the sides of the triangle as \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c\\) as the unknown side. We need to find the integer value of \\(c\\) that satisfies the triangle inequality theorem.\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > c\\) ⟹ \\(4 > c\\) ⟹ \\(c < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + c > 3\\) ⟹ \\(c > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + c > 1\\) ⟹ \\(c > -2\\) (This is always true for positive \\(c\\))\n\nCombining these inequalities, we get \\(2 < c < 4\\). Since \\(c\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(c\\) is 3.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the sides of the triangle\na = 1\nb = 3\n\n# Find the possible integer value for the third side c\npossible_c = []\nfor c in range(1, 5):  # c must be an integer between 1 and 4 (exclusive)\n    if a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a:\n        possible_c.append(c)\n\nprint(possible_c)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe only possible integer value for the third side \\(c\\) that satisfies the triangle inequality theorem is 3. Therefore, the length of the third side is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the length of the third side of a triangle when two sides are given as 1 unit and 3 units, we can use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\):\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nIn this problem, let the sides of the triangle be \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c\\) be the unknown third side. We need to find the integer value of \\(c\\) that satisfies all three inequalities.\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > c\\) ⟹ \\(4 > c\\) ⟹ \\(c < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + c > 3\\) ⟹ \\(c > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + c > 1\\) ⟹ \\(c > -2\\) (which is always true if \\(c\\) is positive)\n\nCombining these inequalities, we get \\(2 < c < 4\\). Since \\(c\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(c\\) is 3.\n\nTherefore, the length of the third side is \\( \\boxed{3} \\). Let's confirm this with a simple Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the two given sides of the triangle\nside1 = 1\nside2 = 3\n\n# Initialize a list to store possible integer values for the third side\npossible_third_sides = []\n\n# Check all possible integer values for the third side within the range (2, 4)\nfor c in range(3, 4):\n    if (side1 + side2 > c) and (side1 + c > side2) and (side2 + c > side1):\n        possible_third_sides.append(c)\n\n# The possible integer value for the third side\nprint(possible_third_sides)\n```", "To determine the length of the third side of a triangle when two sides are known, we can use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the following must be true:\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nGiven the sides of the triangle are 1 unit, 3 units, and an unknown integer \\(x\\), we can set up the following inequalities:\n\n1. \\(1 + 3 > x\\) or \\(4 > x\\) or \\(x < 4\\)\n2. \\(1 + x > 3\\) or \\(x > 2\\)\n3. \\(3 + x > 1\\) (This is always true for positive \\(x\\))\n\nCombining these inequalities, we get \\(2 < x < 4\\). Since \\(x\\) is an integer, the only possible value for \\(x\\) is 3.\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the known sides of the triangle\nside1 = 1\nside2 = 3\n\n# Determine the possible range for the third side using the triangle inequality theorem\n# side1 + side2 > third_side\n# side1 + third_side > side2\n# side2 + third_side > side1\n\n# Calculate the possible range for the third side\nmin_third_side = side2 - side1 + 1  # third_side > side2 - side1\nmax_third_side = side1 + side2 - 1  # third_side < side1 + side2\n\n# Since we need an integer value for the third side\npossible_third_sides = [x for x in range(min_third_side, max_third_side + 1) if 