0(n-2) \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n2. If we denote the missing interior angle by \\(x\\), then the sum of all the interior angles of the polygon is \\(1070^\\circ + x\\).\n3. Using the formula for the sum of interior angles, we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   180(n-2) = 1070 + x\n   \\]\n4. Since \\(x\\) is an interior angle of the polygon, it must be between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can solve for \\(n\\) by finding the integer value that satisfies the equation when \\(x\\) is within this range.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to find the number of sides \\(n\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable for the number of sides\nn = sp.symbols('n', integer=True)\n\n# Define the equation for the sum of interior angles\nsum_of_angles = 180 * (n - 2)\n# The sum of all but one interior angle is 1070 degrees\n# So the sum of all interior angles is 1070 + x, where x is the missing angle\n# Since x is an interior angle, it must be between 0 and 180 degrees\n# We can solve for n by finding the integer value that satisfies the equation when x is within this range\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nequation = sp.Eq(sum_of_angles, 1070 + x)\n\n# Solve the equation for n, considering x is between 0 and 180\nsolutions = []\nfor x_val in range(181):\n    sol = sp.solve(equation.subs(x, x_val), n)\n    if sol and sol[0].is_integer and sol[0] > 0:\n        solutions.append(sol[0])\n\n# The number of sides n must be the same for all valid x values, so we take the first solution\nnumber_of_sides = solutions[0]\nprint(number_of_sides)\n```\n```output\n8\n```\nThe number of sides in the convex polygon is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).", "To determine the number of sides in a convex polygon given that the sum of all but one of its interior angles is \\(1070^\\circ\\), we can use the following steps:\n\n1. **Sum of Interior Angles of a Polygon:**\n   The sum of the interior angles of an \\(n\\)-sided polygon is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   S = 180(n - 2) \\text{