)\ndef p(x):\n    return x**2 - 8*x + 5\n\n# Compute p(3) and p(4)\np_3 = p(3)\np_4 = p(4)\n\n# Compute the ratio p(3) / p(4)\nratio = p_3 / p_4\nprint(ratio)\n```\n```output\n0.9090909090909091\n```\nThe computed ratio $\\frac{p(3)}{p(4)}$ is $\\frac{10}{11}$, which is approximately 0.9090909090909091 when expressed as a decimal.\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{10}{11}}\n\\]", 'To solve this problem, we need to find a quadratic polynomial \\(p(x)\\) with integer coefficients that has \\(4 - \\sqrt{11}\\) as a root. Since the coefficients are integers, the polynomial must also have \\(4 + \\sqrt{11}\\) as a root. This is because the polynomial with integer coefficients must have roots that are conjugates of each other if one of the roots is irrational.\n\nGiven the roots \\(4 - \\sqrt{11}\\) and \\(4 + \\sqrt{11}\\), we can write the quadratic polynomial as:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - (4 - \\sqrt{11}))(x - (4 + \\sqrt{11})) \\]\nfor some integer \\(a\\). Expanding this, we get:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - 4 + \\sqrt{11})(x - 4 - \\sqrt{11}) = a((x - 4)^2 - (\\sqrt{11})^2) = a((x - 4)^2 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 16 - 11) = a(x^2 - 8x + 5) \\]\nSince \\(a\\) is an integer, we can choose \\(a = 1\\) for simplicity (as the value of \\(a\\) will cancel out in the ratio \\(\\frac{p(3)}{p(4)}\\)). So, we have:\n\\[ p(x) = x^2 - 8x + 5 \\]\nNow we need to compute \\(p(3)\\) and \\(p(4)\\):\n\\[ p(3) = 3^2 - 8 \\cdot 3 + 5 = 9 - 24 + 5 = -10 \\]\n\\[ p(4) = 4^2 - 8 \\cdot 4 + 5 = 16 - 32 + 5 = -11 \\]\nFinally, we compute the ratio:\n\\[ \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{-10}{-11} = \\frac{10}{11} \\]\nSo the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{10}{11}}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to find a quadratic polynomial \\( p(x) \\) with integer coefficients that has \\( 4 - \\sqrt{11} \\) as a root. Since the polynomial has integer coefficients, its conjugate \\( 4 + \\sqrt{11} \\) must also be a root. Therefore, the polynomial can be written as:\n\\[ p(x) = a(x - (4 - \\sqrt{11}))(x - (4 + \\