:\n\\[ D = \\frac{|\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{d}|}{|\\mathbf{d}|} \\]\n\nFirst, we compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{d}\\):\n\\[ \\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{d} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -8 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -9 & -2 \\end{vmatrix} = \\mathbf{i}(7 \\cdot (-2) - 5 \\cdot (-9)) - \\mathbf{j}((-8) \\cdot (-2) - 5 \\cdot 2) + \\mathbf{k}((-8) \\cdot (-9) - 7 \\cdot 2) \\]\n\\[ = \\mathbf{i}(-14 + 45) - \\mathbf{j}(16 - 10) + \\mathbf{k}(72 - 14) \\]\n\\[ = 31\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j} + 58\\mathbf{k} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} 31 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 58 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNext, we compute the magnitude of this cross product:\n\\[ |\\mathbf{c} \\times \\mathbf{d}| = \\sqrt{31^2 + (-6)^2 + 58^2} = \\sqrt{961 + 36 + 3364} = \\sqrt{4361} \\]\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\):\n\\[ |\\mathbf{d}| = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-9)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 81 + 4} = \\sqrt{89} \\]\n\nFinally, the distance \\(D\\) is:\n\\[ D = \\frac{\\sqrt{4361}}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4361}{89}} = \\sqrt{49} = 7 \\]\n\nSo the distance between the two parallel lines is \\(7\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the points on the lines\r\na = np.array([3, -10, 1])\r\nb = np.array([-5, -3, 6])\r\n\r\n# Define the direction vectors\r\nd = np.array([2, -9, -2])\r\n\r\n# Vector connecting the points\r\nc = b - a\r\n\r\n# Compute the cross product of c and d\r\ncross_product = np.cross(c, d)\r\n\r\n# Magnitude of the cross product\r\nmagnitude_cross_product = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\r\n\r\n# Magnitude of the direction vector d\r\nmagnitude_d = np.linalg.norm(d)\r\n\r\n# Distance between the parallel lines\r\ndistance = magnitude_cross_product / magnitude_d\r\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n7.0\n```\nThe distance between the two parallel lines is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To find the distance between two parallel lines in 3D space, we can use the following 