= PQ - projection\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the perpendicular vector\ndistance = np.linalg.norm(perpendicular_vector)\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n7.0\n```\nThe distance between the two parallel lines is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To find the distance between two parallel lines, we can use the following method:\n\n1. **Identify the direction vectors of the lines.**\n2. **Find a vector perpendicular to both direction vectors.**\n3. **Compute the shortest distance between the two lines using the perpendicular vector.**\n\nThe two lines are given by:\n\\[ L_1: \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ L_2: \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} + u \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nThe direction vectors are:\n\\[ \\mathbf{d_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ \\mathbf{d_2} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nSince the lines are parallel, \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = 2\\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n\nThe shortest distance \\(d\\) between two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n\\[ d = \\frac{|\\mathbf{n} \\cdot (\\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1})|}{|\\mathbf{n}|} \\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) is a vector perpendicular to both direction vectors, \\(\\mathbf{r_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{r_2}\\) are points on the lines.\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\) are parallel, we can use \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) as the direction vector. The vector \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) can be taken as \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n\nLet's compute this step by step using Python.\n\n1. Find a point on each line.\n2. Compute the vector between these points.\n3. Compute the dot product of this vector with \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n4. Compute the magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n5. Use the formula to find the distance.\n\nLet's implement this in Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Points on the lines\nr1 = np.array([3, -10, 1])\nr2 = np.array([-5, -3, 6])\