.\n\nLet's calculate it step by step using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Points on the lines when the parameter is zero\nr1_0 = np.array([3, -10, 1])\nr2_0 = np.array([-5, -3, 6])\n\n# Direction vectors of the lines\nd1 = np.array([2, -9, -2])\nd2 = np.array([4, -18, -4])\n\n# Compute the cross product of the direction vectors\nn = np.cross(d1, d2)\n\n# Compute the vector r2(0) - r1(0)\nr_diff = r2_0 - r1_0\n\n# Compute the distance using the formula\nnumerator = abs(np.dot(r_diff, n))\ndenominator = np.linalg.norm(n)\ndistance = numerator / denominator\n\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\nnan\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the calculation. Let's break down the steps and ensure we are correctly computing the cross product and the distance. We'll recompute the cross product and the distance step by step.\n\nFirst, let's compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n2 & -9 & -2 \\\\\n4 & -18 & -4\n\\end{vmatrix}\n= \\mathbf{i}((-9)(-4) - (-2)(-18)) - \\mathbf{j}((2)(-4) - (-2)(4)) + \\mathbf{k}((2)(-18) - (-9)(4))\n= \\mathbf{i}(36 - 36) - \\mathbf{j}(-8 + 8) + \\mathbf{k}(-36 + 36)\n= \\mathbf{i}(0) - \\mathbf{j}(0) + \\mathbf{k}(0)\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe cross product is \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which means the lines are coincident or parallel with the same direction. However, since the problem states that the lines are parallel but not coincident, we should recheck the direction vectors. Notice that \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = 2 \\mathbf{d_1}\\), so the lines are indeed parallel with the same direction.\n\nSince the direction vectors are proportional, we can use any point from one line and the direction vector to find the distance. The distance between two parallel lines can also be found using the formula for the distance from a point to a line in 3D.\n\nLet's use the point \\(\\mathbf{r_1}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to find the distance. The distance from a point \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) to a line defined by \\(\\mathbf{r_0}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\frac{|\\mathbf{n} \\cdot (\\mathbf{P} - \\mathbf{r_0})|}{|\\mathbf{n}|} \\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2}\\), but since \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = 2 \\mathbf{d_1}\\), we can use \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n\nLet's recompute the distance using the correct formula.\n```python\n# Since the lines are parallel with the same direction, we can use the point r1_0 and the direction vector d1\n# to find the distance. The distance from a point P to a line defined by r0 and d is given by:\n# Distance = |(P - r0) x d| / |d|\n\n# Point on the first line\nr1_0 = np.array([3, -10, 1])\n# Direction vector of the first line\nd1 = np.array([2, -9, -2])\n\n# Vector from r1_0 to r2_0\nr_diff = r2_0 - r1_0\n\n# Cross product of r_diff and d1\ncross_product = np.cross(r_diff, d1)\n\n# Distance\nnumerator = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\ndenominator = np.linalg.norm(d1)\ndistance = numerator / denominator\n\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'np' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the environment. Let's recompute the distance step by step manually using the correct formula.\n\nThe distance from a point \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) to a line defined by \\(\\mathbf{r_0}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\frac{|\\mathbf{n} \\cdot (\\mathbf{P} - \\mathbf{r_0})|}{|\\mathbf{n}|} \\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2}\\), but since \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = 2 \\mathbf{d_1}\\), we can use \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n\nLet's recompute the distance using the correct formula manually.\n\n1. Compute the vector \\(\\mathbf{r_2}(0