_values[key] = math.log(7 / 3) / math.log(value)\n\nprint(x_values)\n```\n```output\n{\'A\': 1.2004536259234486, \'B\': 3.502962768339671, \'C\': 1.1094472398838856, \'D\': 2.409420839653209, \'E\': 1.4094208396532091}\n```\nThe values of \\(x\\) for each equation are as follows:\n\\[\n\\textbf{(A)}\\ x \\approx 1.200\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(B)}\\ x \\approx 3.503\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(C)}\\ x \\approx 1.109\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(D)}\\ x \\approx 2.409\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(E)}\\ x \\approx 1.409\n\\]\n\nComparing these values, we see that the largest value of \\(x\\) is approximately 3.503, which corresponds to equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\(x\\) for each equation and determine which one has the largest solution for \\(x\\) given \\(0 < r < 3\\). The equations are:\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3(1 + r)^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3(1 + r/10)^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(D)}\\ 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(E)}\\ 3(1 + 1/r)^x = 7\n\\]\n\nWe can isolate \\(x\\) in each equation by rewriting them in the form \\(x = \\frac{\\log(7/3)}{\\log(1 + \\text{coefficient of } r)}\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate \\(x\\) for each equation using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the coefficient r\nx, r = sp.symbols(\'x r\')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = 3 * (1 + r)**x - 7\neq2 = 3 * (1 + r/10)**x - 7\neq3 = 3 * (1 + 2*r)**x - 7\neq4 = 3 * (1 + sp.sqrt(r))**x - 7\neq5 = 3 * (1 + 1/r)**x - 7\n\n# Solve for x in each equation\nsol1 = sp.solve(eq1, x)[0]\nsol2 = sp.solve(eq2, x)[0]\nsol3 = sp.solve(eq3, x)[0]\nsol4 = sp.solve(eq4, x)[0]\nsol5 = sp.solve(eq5, x)[0]\n\n# Simplify the solutions\nsol1 = sp.simplify(sol1)\nsol2 = sp.simplify(sol2)\nsol3 = sp.simplify(sol3)\nsol4 = sp.simplify(sol4)\nsol5 = sp.simplify(sol5)\n\n# Print the s