(b - c) - (b + c)x(b - c) + bc(b - c) - x^2(a - b) + (a + c)x(a - b) - ac(a - b) + x^2(a - c) - (a + b)x(a - c) + ab(a - c). \\]\n\nLet's combine like terms:\n\n\\[ x^2(b - c - a + b + a - c) + x(- (b + c)(b - c) + (a + c)(a - b) - (a + b)(a - c)) + (bc(b - c) - ac(a - b) + ab(a - c)). \\]\n\nSimplifying the coefficients of \\(x^2\\):\n\n\\[ b - c - a + b + a - c = 2b - 2c = 2(b - c). \\]\n\nSince \\(b \\neq c\\), the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is non-zero, so the degree of the polynomial is 2. Therefore, the degree of \\(p(x)\\) is 2. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the symbols\r\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial\r\np = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the polynomial\r\np_simplified = simplify(p)\r\n\r\n# Find the degree of the polynomial\r\ndegree = p_simplified.as_poly().degree()\r\n\r\nprint((p_simplified, degree))\n```\n```output\n(1, 0)\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 1, which is a constant polynomial. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial is 0.\n\nSo, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we need to analyze the expression:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nFirst, let's simplify each term individually. Notice that the denominators are constants and do not affect the degree of the polynomial. So, we can focus on the numerators.\n\n1. The first term \\(\\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)}\\) is a quadratic polynomial in the numerator.\n2. The second term \\(\\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)}\\) is also a quadratic polynomial in the numerator.\n3. The third term \\(\\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}\\) is again a quadratic polynomial in the numerator.\n\nSince each term is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant, the degree of each term is 2. When we add these terms together, the resulting polynomial will still be of degree 2, because the highest degree term from each of the quadratic polynomials will not cancel out.\n\nTo confirm this, let's use Python to simplify and verify the polynomial. We'll use symbolic computation to combine these terms and check the degree of the resulting polynomial.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, a, b, c = sp.symbols('x a b c')\n\n# Define the polynomial terms\nterm1 = (x - b)*(x - c) / ((a - b)*(a - c))\nterm2 = (x - a)*(x - c) / ((b - a)*(b - c))\nterm3 = (x - a)*(x - b) / ((c - a)*(c - b))\n\n# Combine the terms to form the polynomial p(x)\np_x = term1 + term2 + term3\n\n# Simplify the polynomial\np_x_simplified = sp.simplify(p_x)\n\n# Get the degree of the polynomial\ndegree = sp.degree(p_x_simplified, gen=x)\nprint(degree)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nThe output indicates that the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 0. This means that \\( p(x) \\) is a constant polynomial.\n\nTo understand why this is the case, let's consider the values of \\( p(x) \\) at \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\):\n\n1. When \\( x = a \\):\n\\[ p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n2. When \\( x = b \\):\n\\[ p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1. \\]\n\n3. When \\( x = c \\):\n\\[ p(c) = \\frac{(c - b)(c - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. \\]\n\nSince \\( p(x) \\) takes the value 1 at three distinct points \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\), and since \\( p(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree at most 2, it must be the constant polynomial