ual to -3 and solve for \\( b \\):**\n   \\[\n   b - 2 = -3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = -3 + 2 = -1\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and functions\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nb = sp.symbols('b')\nf = x - 3\nq = b * x + 1\n\n# Evaluate q(1)\nq_1 = q.subs(x, 1)\n\n# Substitute q(1) into f(x)\nf_q_1 = f.subs(x, q_1)\n\n# Solve for b given f(q(1)) = -3\nsolution = sp.solve(f_q_1 + 3, b)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[-1]\n```\nThe value of \\( b \\) that satisfies the condition \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Compute \\( q(1) \\) which is \\( b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\).\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\), so \\( f(q(1)) = f(b + 1) \\).\n3. Since \\( f(x) = x - 3 \\), we have \\( f(b + 1) = (b + 1) - 3 = b - 2 \\).\n4. We are given that \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\), so we set \\( b - 2 = -3 \\) and solve for \\( b \\).\n\nLet's implement this step-by-step in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nb = sp.symbols('b')\n\n# Define the function q(x) and f(x)\nq = b * 1 + 1\nf = q - 3\n\n# Given f(q(1)) = -3, we set up the equation\nequation = sp.Eq(f, -3)\n\n# Solve for b\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, b)\nprint(solution[0])\n```\n```output\n-1\n```\nThe value of \\( b \\) that satisfies the equation \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).", "To solve for \\( b \\) given the functions \\( f(x) = x - 3 \\) and \\( q(x) = bx + 1 \\) and the condition \\( f(q(1)) = -3 \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = 1 \\) into \\( q(x) \\) to find \\( q(1) \\).\n2. Substitute the result from step 1 into \\( f(x) \\) and set it equal to \\(-3\\).\n3. Solve the resulting equation for \\( b \\).\n\nLet's break this down step by step.\n\n1. Calculate \\( q(1) \\):\n\\[ q(1) = b \\cdot 1 + 1 = b + 1 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( q(1) \\) into \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ f(q(1)) = f(b 