 observe the pattern:\n\n1. The first two terms of the Fibonacci sequence are 1 and 1.\n2. The next terms are the sum of the previous two terms, taken modulo 4.\n\nWe can generate the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 to see if there is a repeating pattern:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1+1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1+2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2+3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3+1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1+0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0+1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 1+1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 1+2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 2+3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 3+1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence starts repeating every 6 terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0. Therefore, the sequence modulo 4 is periodic with a period of 6. To find the remainder of the 100th term when divided by 4, we need to find the position of the 100th term in the repeating cycle. Since the sequence repeats every 6 terms, we can find the remainder of 100 divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4.\n\\]\n\nThis means that the 100th term has the same remainder as the 4th term in the sequence. From our earlier calculations, we know that the 4th term is 3. Therefore, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code:\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\r\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\r\n    if n == 0:\r\n        return 0\r\n    elif n == 1:\r\n        return 1\r\n    a, b = 0, 1\r\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\r\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\r\n    return b\r\n\r\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\r\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\r\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when 