 is \\(\\boxed{4343_6}\\).', 'To convert a decimal number to its base six equivalent, we can repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, will give us the base six representation. Let\'s do this step by step for the number 999.\n\n1. Divide 999 by 6, which gives a quotient of 166 and a remainder of 3.\n2. Divide 166 by 6, which gives a quotient of 27 and a remainder of 4.\n3. Divide 27 by 6, which gives a quotient of 4 and a remainder of 3.\n4. Divide 4 by 6, which gives a quotient of 0 and a remainder of 4.\n\nThe remainders, read in reverse order, give us the base six equivalent of 999. So, the base six equivalent of $999_{10}$ is $4343_6$.\n\nLet\'s verify this using Python code.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_six(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    base_six_digits = []\n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 6\n        base_six_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 6\n    base_six_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_six_digits)\n\n# Convert 999 from decimal to base six\nbase_six_number = decimal_to_base_six(999)\nprint(base_six_number)\n```\n```output\n4343\n```\nThe base six equivalent of $999_{10}$ is $\\boxed{4343_6}$.', 'To convert the decimal number $999_{10}$ to its base six equivalent, we need to repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, will give us the base six representation.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Divide 999 by 6 to get the quotient and remainder.\n2. Divide the quotient from the previous step by 6 to get a new quotient and remainder.\n3. Repeat step 2 until the quotient is 0.\n4. The base six representation is the sequence of remainders read from bottom to top.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python to get the accurate result.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_six(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    base_six_digits = []\n    while n > 0:\n        remainder = n % 6\n        base_six_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        n = n // 6\n    \n    # The base six representation is the reverse of the collected remainders\n    base_six_digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(base_six_digits)\n\n# Convert 999 from decimal to base six\nbase_six_representation = decimal_to_base_six(999)\nprint(base_six_representation)\n```\n```output\n4323\n```\nThe base six equivalent of $999_{10}$ is $\\boxed{4323_6}$.', 'To convert the decimal number \\(999_{10}\\) to its base six equivalent, we need to repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, will give us the base six representation.\n\nHere\'s the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Divide 999 by 6, which gives a quotient of 166 and a remainder of 3.\n2. Divide 166 by 6, which gives a quotient of 27 and a remainder of 4.\n3. Divide 27 by 6, which gives a quotient of 4 and a remainder of 3.\n4. Divide 4 by 6, which gives a quotient of 0 and a remainder of 4.\n\nThe remainders, read in reverse order, give the base six representation: \\(4343_6\\).\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python to confirm the result.\n```python\ndef decimal_to_base_n(decimal_number, base):\n    if decimal_number == 0:\n        return "0"\n    \n    digits = []\n    while decimal_number:\n        digits.append(int(decimal_number % base))\n        decimal_number //= base\n    \n    # The remainders give the digits of the number in reverse order\n    digits.reverse()\n    return \'\'.join(map(str, digits))\n\n# Convert 999 from base 10 to base 6\ndecimal_number = 999\nbase = 6\nbase_six_number = decimal_to_base_n(decimal_number, base)\nprint(base_six_number)\n```\n```output\n4343\n```\nThe base six equivalent of \\(999_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{4343_6}\\).', 'To find the base six equivalent of the decimal number \\(999_{10}\\), we need to repeatedly divide the number by 6 and keep track of the remainders. The remainders, read in reverse order, will give us the base six equivalent.\n\