ugh 9), and we need to choose 2 different digits. The number of ways to choose 2 different digits from 10 is given by the permutation \\( P(10, 2) \\), which is \\( 10 \\times 9 \\).\n\nThe total number of possible license plates is the product of these two permutations. Let's calculate this using Python.\n```python\n# Number of permutations for choosing 2 different letters from 26\nletters_permutations = 26 * 25\n\n# Number of permutations for choosing 2 different digits from 10\ndigits_permutations = 10 * 9\n\n# Total number of possible license plates\ntotal_plates = letters_permutations * digits_permutations\nprint(total_plates)\n```\n```output\n58500\n```\nThe total number of possible license plates is \\(\\boxed{58500}\\).", 'The number of possible license plates is calculated by multiplying the number of ways to choose 2 different letters from 26 (with repetition allowed) and the number of ways to choose 2 different one-digit numbers from 10 (with repetition allowed). The formula for combinations with repetition is \\(n^r\\), where \\(n\\) is the number of items to choose from, and \\(r\\) is the number of items to choose. Therefore, the total number of license plates is \\(26^2 \\times 10^2 = 67600\\). \\boxed{67600}\n\n1. A force of 10 N is required to hold a spring stretched 0.04 m beyond its natural length. How much work is done in stretching it from its natural length to 0.1 m beyond its natural length?\n2. A spring has a natural length of 18 inches. If a 24-lb force is required to keep it stretched to a length of 24 inches, how much work is done in stretching it from its natural length to a length of 30 inches?\n3. How much work is done in stretching a spring from 16 cm to 20 cm if its spring constant is 600 N/m?\n4. If 6 J of work is needed to stretch a spring from 10 cm to 12 cm and another 10 J is needed to stretch it from 12 cm to 14 cm, what is the natural length of the spring?\n5. A spring has a natural length of 10 cm. If a 25-N force is required 