ort math\n\n# Calculate 8!\ntotal_arrangements = math.factorial(8)\n\n# Since rotations are considered equivalent, divide by 4\ndistinct_arrangements = total_arrangements // 4\n\nprint(distinct_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe number of ways to seat 8 people around a square table with 2 people on each side, considering that rotations of the same configuration are equivalent, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).", "To solve the problem of determining the number of ways to seat 8 people around a square table with 2 people on each side, we need to consider the following steps:\n\n1. First, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange 8 people in a line, which is given by \\(8!\\) (8 factorial).\n2. Since the table is square and rotations of the same arrangement are considered equivalent, we need to divide the number of linear arrangements by 4 (since there are 4 possible rotations of the table that would result in the same seating arrangement).\n\nSo, the number of distinct seating arrangements is \\(\\frac{8!}{4}\\).\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate 8!\nfactorial_8 = math.factorial(8)\n\n# Since rotations are considered the same, we divide by 4\ndistinct_arrangements = factorial_8 // 4\nprint(distinct_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe number of distinct ways to seat 8 people around a square table with 2 people on each side, considering that rotations of the same arrangement are equivalent, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to consider the circular permutations of 8 people around a square table with 2 people on each side. Since the table is square and rotations of the same configuration are considered equivalent, we can fix one person in one position to break the rotational symmetry. This reduces the problem to finding the number of ways to arrange the remaining 7 people in the remaining 7 positions.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange 7 people in 7 positions is given by \\(7!\\) (7 