om \\(C\\) to \\(M\\)), so \\(BM = CM = x\\).\n   - The length of \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) is \\(\\sqrt{x^2 + 11^2}\\).\n\n2. **Use Trigonometry:**\n   - Let \\(\\theta = \\angle BAC\\). Then \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\).\n   - Using the fact that \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\) and \\(\\angle BAC = \\theta\\), we can use the law of sines in triangle \\(BDC\\).\n\n3. **Set Up the Equation:**\n   - In triangle \\(BDC\\), we have \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\), \\(\\angle DBC = \\angle DCB = 90^\\circ - \\theta\\).\n   - Using the law of sines, \\(\\frac{BD}{\\sin(3\\theta)} = \\frac{DC}{\\sin(\\theta)}\\).\n   - Since \\(BD = DC = \\sqrt{x^2 + 1^2}\\), we get \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}}{\\sin(3\\theta)} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2 + 1}}{\\sin(\\theta)}\\).\n   - Simplifying, we get \\(\\sin(3\\theta) = \\sin(\\theta)\\), which implies \\(3\\theta = \\pi - \\theta\\) or \\(3\\theta = \\theta + 2k\\pi\\). Since \\(\\theta\\) is an angle in a triangle, we get \\(3\\theta = \\pi - \\theta\\), so \\(4\\theta = \\pi\\), and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the Perimeter:**\n   - Now we know \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), so \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\).\n   - Using the law of sines, \\(\\frac{x}{\\sin(\\theta)} = \\sqrt{x^2 + 11^2}\\), we get \\(\\frac{x}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} = \\sqrt{x^2 + 11^2}\\).\n   - Simplifying, we get \\(x\\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{x^2 + 121}\\), so \\(2x^2 = x^2 + 121\\), and \\(x^2 = 121\\), so \\(x = 11\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the Perimeter:**\n   - The length of \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) is \\(\\sqrt{11^2 + 11^2} = \\sqrt{242} = 11\\sqrt{2}\\).\n   - The perimeter of triangle \\(ABC\\) is \\(2 \\cdot 11\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\cdot 11 = 22\\sqrt{2} + 22\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python to confirm the result:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Given that theta = pi/4, we can use the law of sines to find x\n# sin(theta) = 1/sqrt(2)\ntheta = sp.pi / 4\nsin_theta = sp.sin(theta)\n\n# Using the law o