f all_formable:\n                valid_ns.append(n)\n    return valid_ns\n\nmax_value = 100  # We can set a reasonable upper limit for n\nmax_stamps = 5\nvalid_ns = find_valid_ns(max_value, max_stamps)\nresult = sum(valid_ns)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems that the initial approach did not yield any valid values for $n$. This suggests that either the logic for checking if $91$ is the greatest value that cannot be formed is incorrect, or there might be a more subtle issue.\n\nLet's refine our approach. We need to ensure that $91$ is the greatest value that cannot be formed using no more than five stamps of denominations $5$, $n$, and $n+1$. This means that $91$ should not be formable, but every value greater than $91$ should be formable.\n\nWe can use a more systematic approach to check the values. Specifically, we can check if $91$ is not formable and if every value from $92$ to a reasonable upper limit (say $100$) is formable. We will also ensure that no value less than $91$ is formable.\n\nHere's the revised code:\n\n```python\ndef can_form(target, denominations, max_stamps):\n    # Use a dynamic programming approach to check if we can form the target value\n    dp = [False] * (target + 1)\n    dp[0] = True  # We can always form 0 with 0 stamps\n\n    for i in range(1, target + 1):\n        for stamp in denominations:\n            if i - stamp >= 0 and dp[i - stamp] and (0 <= (i - stamp) // stamp <= max_stamps):\n                dp[i] = True\n                break\n\n    return dp[target]\n\ndef find_valid_ns(max_value, max_stamps):\n    valid_ns = []\n    for n in range(1, max_value):\n        denominations = [5, n, n + 1]\n        # Check if 91 is not formable\n        if not can_form(91, denominations, max_stamps):\n            # Check if all values from 92 to max_value are formable\n            all_formable = True\n            for i in range(92, max_value + 1):\n                if not can_form(i, denominations, max_stamps):\n                 