mplified = sp.simplify(fraction)\n\n# Add the second term m/25\nz = fraction_simplified + m/25\n\n# Separate the real and imaginary parts\nreal_part = sp.re(z)\nimaginary_part = sp.im(z)\n\n# Set up the equation where the real part is twice the imaginary part\nequation = sp.Eq(real_part, 2 * imaginary_part)\n\n# Solve the equation for m\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, m)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[-1/5]\n```\nThe solution provided is $m = -\\frac{1}{5}$. This means that the value of $m$ that satisfies the condition that the real part of the complex number $z$ is twice the imaginary part is $-\\frac{1}{5}$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{5}}\\), which corresponds to option C.\n\nGiven the function $f(x)=\\begin{cases} x^{2}+2x, & x < 0 \\\\ \\ln (x+1), & x\\geq 0 \\end{cases}$, if the equation $f(x)=x+m$ ($m\\in \\mathbb{R}$) has exactly three distinct real solutions, then the range of $m$ is $\\boxed{(-\\frac{1}{4},0)}$.\nTo solve the problem, we need to find the range of \\(m\\) such that the equation \\(f(x) = x + m\\) has exactly three distinct real solutions. The function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise as follows:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nx^2 + 2x & \\text{if } x < 0 \\\\ \n\\ln(x + 1) & \\text{if } x \\geq 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe need to analyze the equation \\(f(x) = x + m\\) in both cases.\n\n1. For \\(x < 0\\):\n\\[ x^2 + 2x = x + m \\]\n\\[ x^2 + x - m = 0 \\]\nThis is a quadratic equation. For it to have two distinct real solutions, the discriminant must be positive:\n\\[ 1 + 4m > 0 \\]\n\\[ m > -\\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\n2. For \\(x \\geq 0\\):\n\\[ \\ln(x + 1) = x + m \\]\nThis equation can be rewritten as:\n\\[ \\ln(x + 1) - x = m \\]\nTo have exactly one solution in the interval \\(x \\geq 0\\), the function \\(g(x) = \\ln(x + 1) - x\\) must intersect the line \\(y = m\\) exactly once. We need to find the range of \\(m\\) such that this condition is satisfied.\n\nThe function \\(g(x) = \\ln(x + 1) - x\\) has a maximum value