es": 13.8},\n    "12th grade": {"males": 11.6, "females": 8.6}\n}\n\n# Calculate the number of male and female bus riders for each grade\nresults = {}\nfor grade, data in percentages.items():\n    males = total_students * (data["males"] / 100)\n    females = total_students * (data["females"] / 100)\n    thirteen_five_percent_of_females = females * 1.35\n    results[grade] = (males, females, thirteen_five_percent_of_females)\n\n# Find the grade with the closest number of male bus riders to 135% of female bus riders\nclosest_grade = min(results, key=lambda k: abs(results[k][0] - results[k][2]))\nclosest_males = results[closest_grade][0]\nclosest_females = results[closest_grade][1]\nclosest_thirteen_five_percent_of_females = results[closest_grade][2]\n\nprint((closest_grade, closest_males, closest_females, closest_thirteen_five_percent_of_females))\n```\n```output\n(\'11th grade\', 61.8, 41.400000000000006, 55.83000000000001)\n```\nThe grade with the number of male bus riders closest to \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders is the \\(11^{\\text{th}}\\) grade. The number of male bus riders in the \\(11^{\\text{th}}\\) grade is 61.8, and \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders is approximately 55.83. The closest match is in the \\(11^{\\text{th}}\\) grade.\n\nThus, the answer is \\(\\boxed{11^{\\text{th}} \\text{ grade}}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we need to calculate the number of male and female bus riders for each grade and then check which grade has the number of male bus riders closest to \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. Calculate the number of male bus riders for each grade.\n2. Calculate the number of female bus riders for each grade.\n3. Calculate \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders for each grade.\n4. Determine which grade has the number of male bus riders closest to \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python using the given data.\n\n```python\n