 simplify the given expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. Specifically, we combine the \\(x\\) terms and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe \\(x\\) terms are \\(19x\\) and \\(-4x\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x.\\]\n\nThe constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80.\\]\n\nSo the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the expression as a function to verify the simplification\r\ndef simplify_expression(x):\r\n    return 19*x + 1 - 4*x - 81\r\n\r\n# Simplified expression\r\ndef simplified_expression(x):\r\n    return 15*x - 80\r\n\r\n# Test the expressions with a sample value of x\r\nx_value = 10\r\noriginal_value = simplify_expression(x_value)\r\nsimplified_value = simplified_expression(x_value)\r\n\r\nprint((original_value, simplified_value))\n```\n```output\n(70, 70)\n```\nThe original expression and the simplified expression both evaluate to 70 when \\(x = 10\\), confirming that the simplified expression is correct. Therefore, the simplified form of the expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15x - 80}\n\\]", "To simplify the expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. The like terms in this expression are the terms involving \\(x\\) and the constant terms.\n\nThe terms involving \\(x\\) are \\(19x\\) and \\(-4x\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x.\\]\n\nThe constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\). When we combine these, we get:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80.\\]\n\nSo, the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this by writing and running the Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpression = 19*x + 1 - 4*x - 81\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = simplify(expression)\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```out