 vertices of a regular octahedron is constant, we can use the fact that the sum of the squares of the distances from the center of the octahedron to the vertices is \\( 3a^2 \\). However, since we don\'t need the exact value of the constant, we can simply use the relationship that the sum of the squares of the distances from any point to the vertices is constant.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that:\n\\[ x^2 = 324 \\]\n\\[ x = \\sqrt{324} \\]\n\\[ x = 18 \\]\n\nSo the distance from \\( P \\) to the sixth vertex is \\( \\boxed{10} \\).', "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of a regular octahedron. A regular octahedron has 6 vertices and 12 edges, and it can be thought of as two pyramids with a shared square base. The key property we will use is that the sum of the squares of the distances from any point to the vertices of a regular octahedron is constant.\n\nLet's denote the vertices of the octahedron as \\(A, B, C, D, E, F\\) and the point \\(P\\). Let the distance from \\(P\\) to \\(A, B, C, D, E\\) be 3, 7, 8, 9, and 11 respectively. Let the distance from \\(P\\) to \\(F\\) be \\(x\\).\n\nAccording to the property of the regular octahedron, the sum of the squares of the distances from \\(P\\) to the vertices is constant. Therefore, we can write the following equation:\n\n\\[3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 + x^2 = \\text{constant}\\]\n\nSince the sum of the squares of the distances from \\(P\\) to the vertices is constant, we can calculate the sum of the squares of the distances from \\(P\\) to the other vertices and equate it to the sum of the squares of the distances from \\(P\\) to the given vertices.\n\nLet's calculate the sum of the squares of the given distances:\n\n\\[3^2 + 7^2 + 8^2 + 9^2 + 11^2 = 9 + 49 + 64 + 81 + 121 = 324\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[324 + x^2 = \\text{constant}\\]\n\nSince the sum of the squares of the distances from \\(P\\) to all six vertices is constant, we can use the same value for the sum of the squares of the distances