he gray piece is one of the small isosceles triangles\ngray_piece_area = small_triangle_area\n\nprint(gray_piece_area)\n```\n```output\n2.0\n```\nThe area of the gray piece in the Chinese tangram is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) square inches.', 'To solve the problem, let\'s start by understanding the structure of the Chinese tangram and the given square. The square $ABCD$ has an area of 16 square inches, so the side length of the square is $\\sqrt{16} = 4$ inches.\n\nThe tangram consists of seven pieces: five isosceles triangles and one square. The square "e" is one of these pieces and is located in the center of the tangram. The other five pieces are isosceles triangles, and they are arranged around the square "e".\n\nTo find the area of the gray piece, we need to determine the area of the square "e" and then use the fact that the tangram pieces fit together perfectly to form the original square.\n\nThe area of the square "e" is one of the seven equal parts of the total area of the square $ABCD$. Since the total area of the square is 16 square inches, and there are seven pieces, the area of each piece, including the square "e", is $\\frac{16}{7}$ square inches.\n\nHowever, the gray piece is not the square "e". The gray piece is one of the five isosceles triangles. Since the tangram pieces fit together perfectly to form the original square, the area of each isosceles triangle is also $\\frac{16}{7}$ square inches.\n\nTherefore, the area of the gray piece is $\\frac{16}{7}$ square inches.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with a Python code to ensure the accuracy of our reasoning.\n```python\n# Given area of the square ABCD\narea_square = 16\n\n# Number of pieces in the tangram\nnum_pieces = 7\n\n# Area of each piece, including the square "e"\narea_each_piece = area_square / num_pieces\n\n# The gray piece is one of the five isosceles triangles, so its area is the same as each piece\narea_gray_piece = area_each_piece\n\nprint(area_gray_piece)\n```\n```output\n2.2857142857142856\n```\nThe ar