\nn = 1985\n\n# Compute f(n) for n = 1 to 1985\nf1 = 1  # We can assume f1 to be any value, since f(1) = f(1986) will give us the correct sum\nf_values = compute_f(n, f1)\n\n# Calculate the sum of f(1) to f(1985)\nsum_f = sum(f_values[1:n + 1])\nprint(sum_f)\n```\n```output\n-1985\n```\nThe sum of \\(f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985)\\) is \\(\\boxed{-1985}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to first understand the recurrence relation and then find the values of \\(f(n)\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1985. The recurrence relation is given by:\n\n\\[f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\\]\n\nwith the initial condition \\(f(1) = f(1986)\\).\n\nLet's start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence to identify any patterns. We'll use Python to help us with this.\n\n1. Calculate \\(f(2)\\):\n\\[f(2) = (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = 1 - 2f(1)\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(f(3)\\):\n\\[f(3) = (-1)^3 \\cdot 2 - 2f(2) = -2 - 2(1 - 2f(1)) = -2 - 2 + 4f(1) = -4 + 4f(1)\\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(f(4)\\):\n\\[f(4) = (-1)^4 \\cdot 3 - 2f(3) = 3 - 2(-4 + 4f(1)) = 3 + 8 - 8f(1) = 11 - 8f(1)\\]\n\nWe can see that the terms are becoming more complex, so let's use Python to calculate the values of \\(f(n)\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1985 and then sum them up.\n\nWe'll also need to find the value of \\(f(1)\\) using the condition \\(f(1) = f(1986)\\). Let's implement this in Python.\n```python\n# Define the function to compute f(n) using the recurrence relation\ndef compute_f(n, f1):\n    f = [0] * (n + 1)\n    f[1] = f1\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        f[i + 1] = (-1) ** (i + 1) * i - 2 * f[i]\n    return f\n\n# Find the value of f(1) using the condition f(1) = f(1986)\nn = 1986\nf1 = None\nfor f1 in range(-1000, 1000):  # We assume f1 is within a reasonable range for simplicity\n    f = compute_f(n, f1)\n    if f[1] == f[n]:\n        break\n\n# Compute the sum of f(1) + f(2) + ... + f(1985)\nf = compute_f(n, f1)\nresult = sum(f[1:n])\nprint((f1, result))\n```\n```output\n(999, 661)\n```\nThe va