 original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\n### Case 2: \\(b - a = 3\\)\nAdding this equation to \\(a + b = 13\\) gives:\n\\[2b = 16 \\implies b = 8\\]\nSubstituting \\(b = 8\\) into \\(a + b = 13\\) gives:\n\\[a + 8 = 13 \\implies a = 5\\]\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\).\n\nIn both cases, the sum of the original number and the reversed number is \\(85 + 58 = 143\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations and solve them\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b = symbols('a b')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\r\neq2 = Eq(abs(a - b), 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolutions = []\r\nfor eq in [Eq(a - b, 3), Eq(b - a, 3)]:\r\n    sol = solve((eq1, eq), (a, b))\r\n    if sol:\r\n        solutions.append(sol)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\r\nsum_of_numbers = None\r\nfor sol in solutions:\r\n    a_val = sol[a]\r\n    b_val = sol[b]\r\n    original_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\r\n    reversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\r\n    sum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\r\n\r\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number, and \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have two conditions:\n1. The sum of the digits is 13: \\(a + b = 13\\).\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27: \\(10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\).\n\nSimplifying the second condition, we get:\n\\[10a + b - 10b - a = 27 \\implies 9a - 9b = 27 \\implies a - b = 3.\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(a - b = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8.\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\\[8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5.\\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\), and the number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[85 + 58 = 143.\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the equations\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = solution[b]\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. The sum of the digits is given by \\(a + b = 13\\).\n\nWhen the digits are reversed, the number becomes \\(10b + a\\).\n\nThe difference between the original number and the reversed number is given by:\n\\[\n(10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n10a + b - 10b - a = 27\n\\]\n\\[\n9a - 9b = 27\n\\]\n\\[\na - b = 3\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(a - b = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve this system to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nLet's solve these equations using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{a: 8, b: 5}\n```\nThe solution to the system of equations is \\(a = 8\\) and \\(b = 5\\). \n\nThe original number is \\(10a + b = 10 \\cdot 8 + 5 = 85\\).\n\nThe number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10 \\cdot 5 + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[\n85 + 58\n\\]\n\nLet's compute this sum.\n```python\n# Compute the sum of the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * 8 + 5\nreversed_number = 10 * 5 + 8\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. The sum of the digits is \\(a + b = 13\\). When the digits are reversed, the number becomes \\(10b + a\\). According to the problem, the difference between the original number and the reversed number is 27, so we have:\n\n\\[\n(10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n10a + b - 10b - a = 27 \\implies 9a - 9b = 27 \\implies a - b = 3\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[\n8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5\n\\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of the original number and the reversed number is:\n\n\\[\n85 + 58 = 143\n\\]\