1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is the line given by:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x' \\\\ y' \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nExpressing \\(y'\\) in terms of \\(x'\\):\n\\[\nx' = 2x - 1 \\implies x = \\frac{x' + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\ny' = 4x + 1 = 4\\left(\\frac{x' + 1}{2}\\right) + 1 = 2(x' + 1) + 1 = 2x' + 2 + 1 = 2x' + 3\n\\]\n\nSo the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\ny = 2x + 3\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the transformation matrix M\r\nM = sp.Matrix([[4, -1], [2, 1]])\r\n\r\n# Define the parameterized line y = 2x + 1\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nline = sp.Matrix([x, 2*x + 1])\r\n\r\n# Apply the transformation matrix M to the line\r\ntransformed_line = M * line\r\nx_prime, y_prime = transformed_line\r\n\r\n# Express y_prime in terms of x_prime\r\nx_expr = sp.solve(x_prime - (2*x - 1), x)[0]\r\ny_expr = y_prime.subs(x, x_expr)\r\n\r\nprint(y_expr)\n```\n```output\nIndexError: list index out of range\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the code. Let's fix the code and re-run it to ensure we get the correct result. Specifically, we need to correctly express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(x'\\) and then substitute it into the expression for \\(y'\\).\n\nLet's correct the code and re-run it.\n```python\n# Define the transformation matrix M\r\nM = sp.Matrix([[4, -1], [2, 1]])\r\n\r\n# Define the parameterized line y = 2x + 1\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nline = sp.Matrix([x, 2*x + 1])\r\n\r\n# Apply the transformation matrix M to the line\r\ntransformed_line = M * line\r\nx_prime, y_prime = transformed_line\r\n\r\n# Express x in terms of x_prime\r\nx_expr = sp.solve(x_prime - (2