t \\frac{1}{6} = 1 \\]\n\\[ p + q + \\frac{2}{3} = 1 \\]\n\\[ p + q = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nWe also know that when two such dice are rolled, the probability of obtaining a sum of 7 is \\( \\frac{47}{288} \\). The pairs that sum to 7 are (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), and (6,1). Since \\( F \\) and its opposite face sum to 7, let's assume \\( F = 1 \\) and its opposite face is 6. Then the pairs that sum to 7 are (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), and (6,1).\n\nThe probability of rolling a sum of 7 is the sum of the probabilities of these pairs. The probability of rolling (1,6) or (6,1) is \\( p \\cdot q + q \\cdot p = 2pq \\). The probability of rolling (2,5) or (5,2) is \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{36} = \\frac{1}{18} \\). The probability of rolling (3,4) or (4,3) is \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{36} = \\frac{1}{18} \\).\n\nSo the total probability of rolling a sum of 7 is:\n\\[ 2pq + \\frac{1}{18} + \\frac{1}{18} = \\frac{47}{288} \\]\n\\[ 2pq + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288} \\]\n\\[ 2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{1}{9} \\]\n\\[ 2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{32}{288} \\]\n\\[ 2pq = \\frac{15}{288} \\]\n\\[ pq = \\frac{15}{576} \\]\n\\[ pq = \\frac{5}{192} \\]\n\nWe now have the equations:\n\\[ p + q = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ pq = \\frac{5}{192} \\]\n\nWe can solve these equations by substituting \\( q = \\frac{1}{3} - p \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ p \\left( \\frac{1}{3} - p \\right) = \\frac{5}{192} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{p}{3} - p^2 = \\frac{5}{192} \\]\n\\[ 64p - 192p^2 = 5 \\]\n\\[ 192p^2 - 64p + 5 = 0 \\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( p = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 192 \\), \\( b = -64 \\), and \\( c = 5 \\):\n\\[ p = \\frac{64 \\pm \\sqrt{64^2 - 4 \\cdot 192 \\cdot 5}}{2 \\cdot 192} \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{64 \\pm \\sqrt{4096 - 3840}}{384} \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{64 \\pm