= a + b\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n202\n```\nThe sum of $a$ and $b$ is \\(\\boxed{202}\\).\n\nSimplify: $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+2\\sin 610^{\\circ}\\cos 430^{\\circ}}}{\\sin 250^{\\circ}+\\cos 790^{\\circ}}=$\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.\nTo simplify the expression \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+2\\sin 610^{\\circ}\\cos 430^{\\circ}}}{\\sin 250^{\\circ}+\\cos 790^{\\circ}}\\), we need to use trigonometric identities and simplify the angles involved. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. Simplify the angles in the trigonometric functions:\n   - \\(\\sin 610^{\\circ} = \\sin (610^{\\circ} - 360^{\\circ}) = \\sin 250^{\\circ}\\)\n   - \\(\\cos 430^{\\circ} = \\cos (430^{\\circ} - 360^{\\circ}) = \\cos 70^{\\circ}\\)\n   - \\(\\sin 250^{\\circ} = \\sin (270^{\\circ} - 20^{\\circ}) = -\\cos 20^{\\circ}\\)\n   - \\(\\cos 790^{\\circ} = \\cos (790^{\\circ} - 720^{\\circ}) = \\cos 70^{\\circ}\\)\n\n2. Substitute these simplified angles into the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+2\\sin 250^{\\circ}\\cos 70^{\\circ}}}{\\sin 250^{\\circ}+\\cos 790^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+2(-\\cos 20^{\\circ})\\cos 70^{\\circ}}}{-\\cos 20^{\\circ}+\\cos 70^{\\circ}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the numerator using the identity \\(\\cos 70^{\\circ} = \\sin 20^{\\circ}\\):\n   \\[\n   1 + 2(-\\cos 20^{\\circ})\\cos 70^{\\circ} = 1 - 2\\cos 20^{\\circ}\\sin 20^{\\circ} = 1 - \\sin 40^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n   So, the numerator becomes \\(\\sqrt{1 - \\sin 40^{\\circ}}\\).\n\n4. Simplify the denominator:\n   \\[\n   -\\cos 20^{\\circ} + \\cos 70^{\\circ} = -\\cos 20^{\\circ} + \\sin 20^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n\n5. Combine the simplified numerator and denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1 - \\sin 40^{\\circ}}}{-\\cos 20^{\\circ} + \\sin 20^{\\circ}}\n   \\]\n\n6. Use the identity \\(\\sin 40^{\\circ} = 2\\sin 20^{\\circ}\\cos 20^{\\circ}\\):\n   \\[\n   1 - \\sin 40^{\\circ} = 1 - 2\\sin 20^{\\circ}\\cos 20^{\\circ} = \\sin^2 20^{\\circ} + \\cos^2 20^{\\circ} - 2\\sin 20^{\\circ}\\cos 20^{\\circ} = (\\sin 20^{\\circ} - \\cos 20^{\\circ})^2\n   \\]\n   So, the numerator becomes \\(\\sqrt{(\\sin 20^{\\circ} - \\cos 20^{\\circ})^2} = |\\sin 20^{\\circ} - \\cos 20^{\\circ}|\\).\n\n7. Since \\(\\cos 20^{\\circ} > \\sin 20^{\\circ}\\), \\(|\\sin 20^{\\circ} - \\cos 20^{\\circ}| = \\cos 20^{\\circ} - \\sin 20^{\\circ}\\).\n\n8. The expression simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\dfrac{\\cos 20^{\\circ} - \\sin 20^{\\circ}}{-\\cos 20^{\\circ} + \\sin 20^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\cos 20^{\\circ} - \\sin 20^{\\circ}}{-(\\cos 20^{\\circ} - \\sin 20^{\\circ})} = -1\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this step-by-step simplification using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles\nangle_250 = sp.rad(250)\nangle_70 = sp.rad(70)\nangle_20 = sp.rad(20)\n\n# Simplify the expression\nnumerator = sp.sqrt(1 + 2 * sp.sin(angle_250) * sp.cos(angle_70))\ndenominator = sp.sin(angle_250) + sp.cos(sp.rad(790))\nexpression = numerator / denominator\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\nsqrt(2 - 2*sin(7*pi/18))/(-sqrt(2)*(1/2 - sqrt(5)/2) + sqrt(2)*sqrt(5/8 - sqrt(5)/8))\n```\nThe output from the code simplification is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2 - 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{18}\\right)}}{-\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right) + \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}}}\\).\n\nTo further simplify this, let's break it down:\n\n1. **Simplify the numerator:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2 - 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{18}\\right)} = \\sqrt{2 - 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{18}\\right)} = \\sqrt{2(1 - \\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{18}\\right))}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{18}\\right) = \\sin\\left(70^\\circ\\right)\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2(1 - \\sin(70^\\circ))}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplify the denominator:**\n   \\[\n   -\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right) + \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}} = -\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{