unction \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\) is defined for \\( 2 < x < 102 \\) with the exception of \\( x = 12 \\). Therefore, the domain of the function in interval notation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)}\n\\]", "To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. This involves ensuring that the denominator is not zero and that the argument of the logarithm is positive.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Argument of the outer logarithm must be positive:**\n   \\[\n   2 - \\log(x-2) > 0\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\log(x-2) < 2\n   \\]\n   Converting this to an exponential form, we get:\n   \\[\n   x-2 < 10^2 \\implies x-2 < 100 \\implies x < 102\n   \\]\n   So, \\( x \\) must be less than 102.\n\n2. **Argument of the inner logarithm must be positive:**\n   \\[\n   x-2 > 0 \\implies x > 2\n   \\]\n   So, \\( x \\) must be greater than 2.\n\n3. **Denominator must not be zero:**\n   \\[\n   \\log(2 - \\log(x-2)) \\neq 0\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 10^0 \\implies 2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies x-2 \\neq 10^1 \\implies x-2 \\neq 10 \\implies x \\neq 12\n   \\]\n   So, \\( x \\) must not be equal to 12.\n\nCombining all these conditions, the domain of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\\[\n2 < x < 102 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x \\neq 12\n\\]\nIn interval notation, this is:\n\\[\n(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = (2 - x) / sp.log(2 - sp.log(x - 2, 10), 10)\n\n# Find the domain of the function\ndomain = sp.calculus.util.continuous_domain(f, x, sp.S.Reals)\nprint(domain)\n```\n```output\nUnion(Interval.open(2, 12), Interval.open(12, 102))\n```\nThe domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2